./LOGBOOK.md:167:### 1943 — libssh2 adapter closes review round 5 fatal teardown and ownership gaps (TASK-260715-1ozsb6)
./LOGBOOK.md:168:- ROOT CAUSE: Fatal socket failures were mapped inconsistently and could leave an apparently usable transport; automatic keepalive used its reply deadline while waiting behind KEX; teardown queues and task snapshots could grow or report optimistic zeros; and one-shot channel cleanup could discard a live libssh2 pointer after `EAGAIN`.
./LOGBOOK.md:169:- REWORK: One privacy-safe failure path now tears down every lane-fatal socket error. Automatic keepalive defers behind an active rekey and starts a fresh reply deadline after gate admission. Session/channel admission is capped, shared drains and teardown use single-flight tasks, and teardown cancels and joins all owned work before clearing handles. Channel close/free remains tracked until libssh2 confirms disposal; cleanup failure retains ownership for session teardown.
./LOGBOOK.md:170:- NONBLOCKING CLEANUP: A terminal socket enters an abort-drain mode that feeds EOF to receives and consumes only libssh2's already-retained outbound bytes. This lets the engine complete its nonblocking destructors without network I/O or fabricated operation success. Session destruction is bounded; failure remains explicit and keeps ownership visible rather than claiming a closed baseline.
